// The system requirements of DriftSpec, in DriftSpec. Package level: what the
// tool promises at its boundaries (the ds command, the export, the editors).
// One capability per requirement, in the positive; the design and the
// verifications refine and verify them from the modules.

package driftspec {

  requirement SREQ-00001-1 {
    status: approved
    title: The system shall load a workspace of .ds files into one model in which every entity has one identity
    text:
      A workspace is a directory; its files are read whole and in any order.
      A design entity is identified by its full name (package with @, modules
      with ::) and a process artefact by its id with its revision. A second
      declaration of one identity is reported; packages and modules reopen
      across files.
    rationale:
      A specification that lives per file cannot say that two files talk about
      the same thing. Identity across the workspace is what makes references,
      diagrams and diffs mean the same entity everywhere.
    tags: workspace, identity
  }

  requirement SREQ-00002-1 {
    status: approved
    title: The system shall report every incoherence of the model as a finding with a code, a severity and a location
    text:
      A reference nobody declares, a duplicate identity, a template argument
      outside its bound, a composition cycle, an operator refined but not
      declared, a message to an operation that does not exist, a link to an
      artefact revision the workspace no longer declares: each is one finding
      at the place it was written. Loading never fails on a dangling
      reference, only on syntax.
    rationale:
      The tool's value is saying where the model contradicts itself. A tool
      that stopped at the first problem, or that failed to load a workspace
      with a missing declaration, could not be used while the design is being
      written.
    tags: coherence, findings
  }

  requirement SREQ-00003-2 {
    status: approved
    title: The system shall keep requirements, constraints and verifications as artefacts linked to the design by revision
    text:
      Requirements, constraints and verifications are declared in the same
      language as the design, with an id that carries their revision. Every
      link to an artefact names a revision; nesting gives the relation
      (a requirement in a requirement refines it, a verification in a
      requirement verifies it) and annotations add relations from elsewhere:
      a package (an HLD box) and a class realise a requirement the same way
      (revision 2, decided 2026-10-26).
    rationale:
      The design and the artefacts about it belong to one model: a box or a
      class realises a requirement, a constraint binds it, a verification proves it.
      Pinning links to a revision is what lets the tool tell who stayed behind
      when an artefact moved.
    tags: requirements, traceability
  }

  requirement SREQ-00004-1 {
    status: approved
    title: The system shall model behaviour as sequences and state machines bound to the static entities they describe
    text:
      A sequence or a state machine is the behaviour of a method or a class and
      is identified by it. Its participants are the static context of the
      subject: self, the attributes of the class and the parameters of the
      method; only what the behaviour creates is declared. A message is checked
      against the operation it names and its arity; a message to an operation
      that has a behaviour expands to it.
    rationale:
      A sequence diagram drawn apart from the classes it names drifts from them
      without anyone noticing. Bound by identity, the behaviour is checked like
      the rest of the model and the abstraction level of a diagram comes from
      the architecture instead of from the author.
    tags: behaviour, sequences
  }

  requirement SREQ-00005-1 {
    status: approved
    title: The system shall draw any selection of the model as a diagram, in SVG and in the terminal, adapting to a given width
    text:
      A diagram selects entities and decides what to print; it never draws.
      The same selection is drawn as an SVG that a browser, an editor or a
      document embeds, and as box-drawing text for a terminal, from one layout
      that wraps to the width it is given. The SVG carries stable ids, links by
      identity, tooltips and the interaction to fold and focus.
    rationale:
      If the drawing is a projection, it cannot drift from the model, and the
      reader can ask for the view that explains what they need rather than
      maintain pictures by hand.
    tags: render, diagrams
  }

  requirement SREQ-00013-1 {
    status: approved
    title: The system shall lay out the whole model once, every box nested in its parent, so a viewer can zoom from the system down to a member
    text:
      One scene of the whole workspace: every package, module, interface,
      class, function, enum and member has one place, inside the box that
      holds it and apart from its siblings, computed once so that nothing
      moves while a reader zooms. It comes in two variants, with every member
      and with public members only, and as JSON from the command line.
    rationale:
      Owner, 2026-11-05 (DS-67): one diagram of the whole model, information
      shown on demand, instead of many diagrams drawn apart. Depth is the
      level of abstraction; the scene is what makes it possible.
    tags: render, scene
  }

  requirement SREQ-00020-1 {
    status: approved
    title: The system shall install its command line beside its Python package with one pip install
    text:
      pip install driftspec puts the ds command in the environment's scripts, next to the package,
      driftspec-lsp and driftspec-live: one installation gives every way of using the engine,
      without a compiler on the reader's side once the release ships built wheels.
    rationale: Owner, 2026-11-18 (DS-303): the command line was only to be had by building it with CMake.
    tags: python, cli, release
  }

  requirement SREQ-00015-1 {
    status: approved
    title: The system shall serve its engine in process to Python, as a package to depend on
    text:
      pip install driftspec gives a package whose engine runs in the same process: load a
      workspace, from a directory or from the texts an editor holds, into a typed model; check
      it; draw a diagram; write the viewer. One native module serves every Python from 3.10
      on, and it speaks the JSON of the command line, so a tool sees one contract.
    rationale:
      Owner, 2026-11-06 (DS-84): argos reads DriftSpec designs through a plugin that ran the
      binary and parsed its output; a dependency with a pinned version replaces the search for
      a binary on the PATH, and a language server needs the engine at hand.
    tags: python, api
  }

  requirement SREQ-00016-1 {
    status: approved
    title: The system shall serve .ds files to an editor through the language server protocol
    text:
      driftspec-lsp checks the workspace a file belongs to on every change, with the buffers
      the editor holds, and publishes each file's findings; it leads from a name to its
      declaration and to what refers to it, says what an entity is, completes with what the
      grammar takes and the names the model declares, and draws the entity or the diagram
      under the cursor, as text or in the viewer.
    rationale:
      Owner, 2026-11-06 (DS-85): a server in Python on the Python face; the engine stays C++.
    tags: lsp, editor
  }

  requirement SREQ-00017-1 {
    status: approved
    title: The system shall show a design live while it is written, to a reader and to an agent who point at the same identities
    text:
      A session over a workspace follows its .ds files: on every change the reader sees the design
      again where they were looking, with what changed marked and the findings beside it. The one
      who writes, a person or an agent, takes the reader to an identity, highlights identities and
      puts notes on them; the reader's selection reaches the one who writes. The same contract serves
      a browser page and an editor's panel, and an agent through MCP.
    rationale:
      Owner, 2026-11-07 (DS-95, DS-97): DriftSpec as the medium in which a person and an agent talk
      about a design and watch it change; tried end to end in a spike before consolidating.
    tags: live, viewer, mcp
  }

  requirement SREQ-00018-2 {
    status: approved
    title: The system shall be driven by one command, ds, with a verb for each use case and an exit code that names how it ended
    text:
      A person at a terminal and a script drive the tool the same way: ds, a verb (check, export,
      render, query, manual), the workspace and the options of that verb. What is asked for
      goes to standard output, why nothing was done to standard error, and the exit code names the
      outcome of the use case the verb serves: 0 when it went well, another for each way it may
      end. The command reads the arguments, loads the workspace and hands it to whoever answers;
      nothing else in the tool knows of a command line.
    rationale:
      The command line was there and nothing asked for it: the use cases say what goes in and what
      comes out, the operations of the cli interface say how each is typed, and no requirement said
      that a command exists and owns the arguments and the exits (found by argos as diagrams with no
      requirement, DS-137). It is the owner of the app module in the sense of bottom-up ownership:
      why the module exists; what it does are the use cases, which hang from the requirements they
      serve.
      Revision 2: ds diff left the tool before its first release (Owner, 2026-11-18, DS-277).
    tags: cli, app
  }

  requirement SREQ-00019-1 {
    status: approved
    title: The system shall come with a page that edits a workspace in a browser, draws its diagrams beside the text as it is typed, and teaches the language in guided lessons in English and Spanish
    text:
      The playground is a static page over the engine compiled to WebAssembly (REQ-00113-1): the
      files of a workspace in a tree, the text in an editor, one pane per diagram and a table of the
      requirements beside it, the problems below, and a click on a drawing leading to where it is
      written. Fifteen lessons, from the page itself to rules, grow one example from a class to a
      small system: the lesson types its own code in front of the reader, asks the reader to write
      the rest and says when the engine sees it done, offers to write a step for a reader who is
      stuck, and starts again whenever it is chosen. Each lesson is a test too: replayed with its
      solutions, it ends with a clean model.
    rationale:
      Owner, 2026-11-15 (DS-201): the way into DriftSpec for a person who has never seen it, and the
      place to review the syntax by writing the tutorial from the bottom up. Designed in the spike
      spike/wasm-playground and consolidated in DS-202, DS-203 and DS-204.
    tags: playground, browser, tutorial
  }

  requirement SREQ-00014-1 {
    status: approved
    title: The system shall carry its own manual: how each kind of the language is written, what each finding means and what each command's exit codes say
    text:
      ds manual prints, from the tool itself and with no workspace: an index
      of the commands and the kinds; for each kind, how it is written, its
      fields, flags and relations, and a minimal example that checks clean;
      for each finding code, what it means and what to do; the annotations
      that mean something; the commands with their exit codes, which every
      command's help repeats. The manual is generated from the metamodel and
      held to the engine, so it cannot drift from the language.
    rationale:
      Owner, 2026-11-06, like argos: a newcomer agent with only the binary
      understood what ds does (7/10) but could not write a design without
      guessing its syntax (3/10, some 35 attempts); the metamodel named every
      kind and field but not how any of them is written.
    tags: manual, cli, language
  }

  requirement SREQ-00006-1 {
    status: approved
    title: The system shall export the model, with its relations and findings, for other tools
    text:
      The whole model, every entity with its fields and flags, every relation
      with its kind and multiplicity and every finding with its location, is
      available as JSON from the command line, and the drawing of a diagram is
      available as the render-IR of the plantuml family.
    rationale:
      argos compares the declared design with the code and the tests; it needs
      the model, not the files. The IR keeps the family's renderers
      interchangeable.
    tags: export, argos
  }

  requirement SREQ-00008-1 {
    status: approved
    title: The system shall provide its grammar to editors so that a .ds file is parsed, highlighted and folded as it is typed
    text:
      The language is defined by a tree-sitter grammar with its highlight and
      fold queries, built from this repository, and an editor loads them as it
      loads any other language's.
    rationale:
      A specification language that is not supported where it is written will
      not be written.
    tags: grammar, editors
  }

  requirement SREQ-00009-2 {
    status: approved
    title: The system shall model the static design: packages, modules, classes with their members, ownership, inheritance, templates, operators and boundaries
    text:
      A package is the black box and declares the boundaries it exposes; a
      package holds packages, written nested or reopened by its full path,
      every level adding its name with @, so that one workspace models one
      system of several packages and the level of an artefact follows from
      where it is declared (revision 2, decided 2026-10-26); a
      module is a namespace and opens the white box; classes, data classes
      and enumerations carry attributes and methods with visibility,
      modifiers and types; an attribute owns, references or shares what it
      holds; a class inherits, is parameterised with bounds, and declares its
      operators in its header. Interfaces, abstract classes and value types
      are derived from the members, never declared.
    rationale:
      This is the vocabulary a design is written in. What is derived cannot
      contradict what is declared, and the ownership written on the
      attribute is what the drawings and the checks read.
    tags: metamodel, static
  }

  requirement SREQ-00010-1 {
    status: approved
    title: The system shall provide a diagram kind for each view of the model: class, requirement, sequence and state
    text:
      A diagram is a selection of the model with what to print: a class
      diagram over the static design, a requirement diagram over the
      artefacts and what refines, verifies and realises them, a sequence
      diagram over a behaviour at the level asked for, a state diagram over
      the state machine of a class. A diagram never draws; the model draws
      itself through it.
    rationale:
      Each part of the metamodel needs its view, and keeping the views as
      selections is what keeps them from drifting from the model.
    tags: diagrams, views
  }

  requirement SREQ-00011-1 {
    status: approved
    title: The system shall answer questions about the model: what exists, what an entity relates to, what a diagram shows
    text:
      From the command line and from any host, the model can be asked: the
      diagrams a workspace declares, the entities of a module, the relations
      an entity has and in which direction, the requirements a class
      realises and the verifications that prove them, the behaviours of a
      method, the findings of a file. An answer names entities by their
      identity, so that it can be followed by another question or by a
      drawing.
    rationale:
      A model is only as useful as the questions it answers without reading
      the files. The same mechanism serves a person at the terminal, an
      editor, an agent and argos, and the drawings become one answer among
      others.
    tags: query, model
  }

  requirement SREQ-00012-1 {
    status: approved
    title: The system shall define a formal language in which a software design is specified as text: its high-level design, its low-level design, the artefacts about it and the views of it
    text:
      The language has a grammar that reads every specification unambiguously,
      a vocabulary of entities with one identity each, and rules that give
      every construct one meaning; a workspace of files written in it is one
      specification. The rules are written as requirements and constraints of
      the language, each decided with its rationale and fixed by an oracle
      corpus.
    rationale:
      Everything else the system promises, loading, checking, drawing,
      comparing, asking, exists because a specification means one thing.
      Without a formal language the model would be whatever the tool happened
      to read.
    tags: language, grammar
  }

  constraint CST-00011-1 {
    status: approved
    category: release
    title: Everything DriftSpec ships shall carry its licence, Apache 2.0 with its notice, and the grammar its own, MIT
    text:
      LICENSE is the Apache License 2.0; NOTICE names the holder, ARGOSQA SLU (deriva), and points at
      the third-party notices. grammar/LICENSE is the MIT License, as tree-sitter grammars are; the
      grammar's manifest says MIT. The wheel and the site carry the three files, and so does every
      binary a release attaches.
    rationale:
      Owner, 2026-11-18 (DS-287): Apache 2.0 for its patent grant and its trademark clause, MIT for
      the grammar as is the custom of the ecosystem, held by the company.
    tags: release, licence
  }

  constraint CST-00010-1 {
    status: approved
    category: release
    title: Everything DriftSpec ships shall carry the notices of the third-party code compiled into it
    text:
      THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md names each library the build fetches, with the version built and its
      licence text, and the Emscripten runtime the WebAssembly engine is made with; it goes in the
      wheel and in the site, and with every binary a release attaches.
    rationale:
      Owner, 2026-11-18 (DS-284), audit for the first release: tree-sitter, nlohmann json and miniz
      (MIT) and CLI11 (BSD-3-Clause) ask that their notice go with any copy.
    tags: release, licence
  }

  constraint CST-00001-1 {
    status: approved
    category: performance
    title: Every command of the tool shall complete in under one hundred milliseconds on a workspace of a hundred classes
    text:
      Loading, checking, exporting, diffing and drawing a workspace of a
      hundred classes with their relations, behaviours and diagrams completes
      in under one hundred milliseconds on a development machine, so that an
      editor preview or an agent loop can redraw on every change.
    rationale:
      Measured at the proof of concept: five milliseconds for the engine's own
      design, eight for a synthetic hundred-class document. A budget keeps the
      property as the tool grows; it applies to every command, which is why it
      is a constraint and not a capability.
    tags: performance
  }
}

diagram REQ_System {
  kind: requirement
  include: driftspec@*           // every artefact of the package: the system requirements and what hangs from them
}
